## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## docs-refresh  (v3 — one capable agent + a mission + TRUTH gates
##                only; the scan is advisory, never an obligation)
##
## THE MISSION is an EXHAUSTIVE DOC-ALIGNMENT campaign: converge the
## documentation to the actual current state of the repo. Docs follow
## code, exhaustively — never the reverse. Two halves, BOTH doc-side:
## repair documentation that is stale or obsolete, and WRITE the
## documentation that is missing. All edits land as semantic
## `docs(scope):` commits in stride. When a repo has NO docs in scope
## yet, an authoring pass bootstraps an initial set first
## (DEFAULT-CREATE, unchanged since v0.3.0).
##
## WHY v3 (the Billy/Willy paradigm — "a capable agent + a mission +
## truth gates only; excess determinism is counter-productive"). The
## v2.x lineage kept growing deterministic machinery in FRONT of the
## agent: an anchor scanner (file_ref / cli_flag / diagnostic /
## symbol regexes) promoted to an OBLIGATION GENERATOR, mechanical
## convergence conditions (`coverage_pct >= target`,
## `undocumented_count == 0`), and a severity/chunking pipeline that
## decided the agent's plan for it. Two live runs against a real repo
## (019f8af6 / 019f8b50, 2026-07-22) measured the cost: ~200
## false-positive cli_flag candidates (git/docker/gh flags quoted in
## doc examples) adjudicated one by one — ~40 of 44 minutes burned;
## successive patches (foreign-flag heuristic, cli_names derivation,
## pseudo-doc chunk slots) were symptom treatment; the priority/chunk
## pipeline buried the real work (enrichment) behind scanner noise
## for 4 passes; final ratio 55 min / ~$10 for +61 lines, half the
## wall-clock re-building the repo for docs-only commits. The scanner
## metrics had become the goal: the agent served the scanner instead
## of exploring the repo.
##
## THE v3 SHAPE keeps ONLY truth-oracle determinism:
##   scan_hints    — ONE deterministic producer of ADVISORY hints: a
##                   compact report (probable drift sorted by signal:
##                   missing repo-rooted paths, dead internal links /
##                   anchors; potentially undocumented code areas;
##                   coverage telemetry) handed to the campaign as
##                   HELP. The campaign is free to use it, contradict
##                   it, and above all to explore BEYOND it (read the
##                   code, find the drift no scan can see). Hints are
##                   never a gate and never an obligation. High
##                   precision by construction: a cited path is only
##                   checkable when its first segment exists as a
##                   repo directory — foreign-tool examples (docker /
##                   gh flag lines, other repos' layouts) are
##                   silently skipped, not surfaced as noise. On a
##                   repo with nothing scannable the producer
##                   degrades silently to an empty report + an
##                   explicit "explore directly" note. Also carries
##                   the noop short-circuit (cache git_head == HEAD,
##                   clean tree, no explicit request → done with zero
##                   LLM cost) and the DEFAULT-CREATE routing.
##   campaign      — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools).
##                   Mission = align the docs exhaustively on the
##                   état de l'art of the code; adjudicate every
##                   issue to exactly one of FOUR outcomes
##                   (fix+commit / dismiss+ledger / promise+ledger /
##                   code-bug→board); a living todo list + one
##                   semantic commit per doc, in stride. git IS the
##                   durable state — a re-dispatch re-hints and the
##                   campaign continues from the commits earlier
##                   passes banked.
##   scope_check   — deterministic writeable-set containment (docs +
##                   cache + opted-in go_comment_globs) vs the run
##                   base. TRUTH: the bot must not touch code.
##   verify_build  — the stack-agnostic build/test gate shared with
##     + verify_run   the other v2 bots (ADR-044): the agent writes
##                   the repo's REAL build+test into an out-of-tree
##                   verify.sh; a deterministic tool re-runs it on
##                   the real exit code. verify_precheck keeps the
##                   docs-only fast path (green verdict reused when
##                   every change since the green HEAD is `.md`).
##                   TRUTH: the tree must still build.
##   gate          — deterministic compute:
##                   converged = verify.passed ∧ scope_ok ∧
##                   campaign.docs_aligned — NOTHING ELSE. Scanner
##                   counts are telemetry (report, events), never
##                   conditions.
##   ledgers       — the dismissals ledger (vars.dismissed_path) and
##                   the promises ledger (<scratch>/promises.json)
##                   are the campaign's cross-pass MEMORY: its own
##                   adjudications persist (scan_hints excludes
##                   ledger entries; the PR tail reports promises).
##                   Memory, never a cage.
##
## RETIRED FROM v2.5 (git history has the design): scan_docs +
## scan_code_surface + build_manifest (the anchor scanner / manifest /
## reverse-containment machinery — replaced by the single advisory
## scan_hints), the coverage_pct / undocumented_count gate conditions,
## the severity/chunking pipeline (max_drift_candidates /
## max_review_chunk_docs / cli_surface_globs / diagnostic_surface_globs
## / include_unverifiable_symbols / coverage_target_pct / enrich /
## enrich_area_depth vars), and the per-doc SHA pre-verification cache
## (the inter-run cache keeps only git_head for the noop gate).
## 15 nodes → 13; ~1400 lines of embedded python → ~350.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (scan_hints→campaign→scope_check→verify_precheck[→verify_build
## →verify_run]→gate→scan_hints) is capped by `max_passes`; on
## exhaustion it ships what is banked (every committed doc alignment
## is real, landed work). `iterion validate` reports NO undeclared
## cycle (author_rescan(1) is the one other declared loop).
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): universal doc_globs defaults, no language /
## ecosystem / layout literal anywhere in vars, commands or schemas;
## the stack knowledge lives in the bundled skills; the hint
## heuristics degrade silently when nothing is derivable.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Doc footprint scope (comma-separated globs). scan_hints
  ## enumerates files matching these globs each pass. Hidden /
  ## vendored / runtime dirs are excluded unconditionally via
  ## `excluded_dirs`; the bot's own bundle is excluded when
  ## `bundle_self_path` is set.
  ## UNIVERSAL DEFAULT: docs-refresh documents ANY repo, so the
  ## default footprint is the conventions common to most projects
  ## (root + nested READMEs, a docs/ tree, CLAUDE.md). Repo-specific
  ## doc locations are added per-run via --var, never baked into the
  ## catalog default.
  doc_globs: string = "README.md,docs/**/*.md,**/README.md,CLAUDE.md,**/CLAUDE.md"

  ## Go code comment scope (comma-separated globs). Empty (default)
  ## means: do NOT audit Go function/package comments. Non-empty
  ## widens the campaign's writeable set (and scope_check's
  ## allow-list) to comment-only edits in those files.
  go_comment_globs: string = ""

  ## Optional free-text notes pinning the campaign's attention.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## Auto-exclusion. When non-empty, scan_hints excludes any file
  ## whose path starts with this prefix from the doc footprint. Set
  ## to the bot's own bundle dir when running docs-refresh on a repo
  ## that contains its source; otherwise leave empty.
  bundle_self_path: string = ""

  ## Incremental mode. When non-empty, scan_hints computes
  ## `git diff <diff_since>...HEAD --name-only` and surfaces the
  ## result as `recently_changed_code_files` — a prioritisation HINT
  ## for the campaign, never a scope reduction. Useful for nightly /
  ## per-PR runs (`--var diff_since=main`).
  diff_since: string = ""

  ## Hard-excluded directories. Comma-separated list of directory
  ## names whose presence anywhere in a path disqualifies it from the
  ## doc footprint (regardless of `doc_globs`). Defaults exclude
  ## runtime / scratch / vendor / artifact dirs that should never be
  ## audited.
  excluded_dirs: string = ".iterion,.works,.claude,vendor,node_modules,.git,dist,build,out"

  ## Cap on the advisory hints list handed to the campaign (context
  ## bound — telemetry counts are computed BEFORE truncation, so the
  ## report stays honest about what was cut).
  max_hints: int = 120

  ## Identifier of the tracker issue this run was dispatched from.
  ## Used by mark_issue_for_review at the end of the workflow to
  ## transition the issue out of `ready`. Filled by the dispatcher
  ## via `vars.issue_id: "{{issue.identifier}}"`. Empty when run
  ## outside the dispatcher — mark_issue_for_review is then a no-op.
  issue_id: string = ""

  ## Inter-run noop cache. JSON file storing the git HEAD the docs
  ## were last aligned to; a matching HEAD on a clean tree with no
  ## explicit request (issue_id) short-circuits the whole run
  ## (noop_skip) with zero LLM cost — killing wasteful scheduled runs
  ## on an unchanged repo. Out-of-tree under the project scratch dir
  ## (a repo-root dotfile would not survive `worktree: auto`); under
  ## a sandbox scratch is per-run, so the cache simply no-ops (safe
  ## degradation — noop never wrongly skips). Empty disables it.
  audit_cache_path: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/docs-refresh-cache.json"

  ## DEFAULT-CREATE: when scan_hints finds ZERO documentation files
  ## in scope, the `author_docs` node creates this directory and
  ## authors an initial documentation set there from the code; the
  ## run then re-scans (author_rescan) and the normal campaign
  ## polishes + commits it. Repo-relative; DEFAULT "docs" (covered by
  ## the default doc_globs). If you point it OUTSIDE the default
  ## doc_globs, widen doc_globs too.
  docs_dir: string = "docs"

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic
  ## gate's verify script/log and the promises ledger. The engine
  ## resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/
  ## scratch non-sandboxed and to the container-writable
  ## /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox. Keeping it OUT of the
  ## target worktree is the repo-agnostic rule.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/docs-refresh"

  ## DISMISSALS LEDGER — the campaign's cross-pass MEMORY. JSON array
  ## of {doc, kind, value, reason} entries the campaign adjudicated
  ## as not-worth-acting-on (false positives, internal-only areas,
  ## recorded promises). scan_hints excludes ledger entries from
  ## every later pass so a settled adjudication never re-surfaces —
  ## persistence of the agent's own judgment, never a cage. Out of
  ## tree like scratch_dir; empty disables the exclusion.
  dismissed_path: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/docs-refresh/dismissed.json"

  ## BASELINE (G5). Known pre-existing build/test failures the
  ## campaign must SKIP rather than burn budget proving they aren't
  ## its own — docs-refresh rarely trips this (docs-only edits), but
  ## when go_comment_globs opts comment edits in, the build gate runs
  ## and a red base tree must not absorb the run. Empty = establish
  ## once cheaply, then skip what predates the run.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each
  ## pass is one hints→campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping
  ## what is banked) when the docs are reported aligned + the gates
  ## are green, or when this cap is hit.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds
  ## the loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is
  ## N+1 — max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles.
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── PR finalization (opt-in) ─────────────────────────────────
  ## Same contract as feature-dev/app-dev: when open_mr=true, after
  ## the run finishes (converged OR max_passes exhausted with banked
  ## commits), finalize_mr pushes the doc-alignment series and opens
  ## the pull request (PR; merge request on GitLab). Requires a git
  ## remote + a push credential (forge_token secret, *_TOKEN env, or
  ## host gh auth) — the deterministic forge_auth_probe skips the
  ## tail cleanly when none exists. The noop_skip path never reaches
  ## this tail, and finalize_mr refuses to open an empty PR.
  open_mr: bool = false
  mr_branch: string = ""
  mr_base: string = ""
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + PR creation + the issue back-link.
  ## Mounted as a read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from;
  ## never read into a prompt. optional:true so local runs (no forge
  ## token bound) and host-authenticated gh/glab setups work
  ## unchanged.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior technical writer-engineer running a DOC-ALIGNMENT
  campaign — working EXACTLY as you would in a productive, human-driven
  session. You have the repo in front of you and full tools (read, edit,
  shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass end to end.

  THE MISSION: align the documentation, exhaustively, with the actual
  current state of the code — the état de l'art of the repo. DOCS
  FOLLOW CODE, never the reverse. Both halves of the work are
  DOC-SIDE:
    - REPAIR what is stale or obsolete: update every documented claim
      the code has moved past.
    - WRITE what is missing: document capabilities, surface, and code
      areas that exist in the repo but appear in no doc.
  You correct and author documentation, never code.

  LOAD YOUR SKILLS FIRST. The bundle ships six skills under
  `.claude/skills/` describing the discipline you MUST follow:
    - `docs-refresh.md` — operating playbook, the why and the
      inviolable rules
    - `doc-mismatch-taxonomy.md` — the mismatch kinds that tell you
      what a real drift looks like
    - `doc-enrichment.md` — what deserves documentation, where to put
      it, how to write it, when to dismiss instead, and how to tell
      obsolete prose from a deliberate (unfulfilled) promise
    - `doc-scope-enumeration.md` — footprint + writeable-set contract
    - `doc-verification-checklist.md` — the verification preamble to
      run before declaring anything aligned
    - `anti-facade-fix-rules.md` — what a façade fix looks like

  THE SCAN IS AN AID, NOT YOUR SCOPE. Your user prompt carries an
  ADVISORY report from a deterministic scan: probable drift sorted by
  signal (paths cited in docs that no longer exist, dead internal
  links/anchors), code areas no doc mentions, and coverage telemetry.
  Use it as a starting point — it is cheap, high-precision help. But
  it is NOT your working set and NOT a checklist you owe anyone:
    - You are FREE to contradict a hint (verify first, then dismiss
      it to the ledger with a reason).
    - You MUST explore BEYOND it: read the code, run the entry
      points' --help, diff recent changes, hunt the semantic drift no
      regex can see (wrong defaults, stale behaviour descriptions,
      outdated examples, missing capability docs). The scan sees
      paths and links; YOU see meaning. Most real drift is semantic.
    - An empty or degraded report ("nothing scannable") means nothing
      about alignment — it means you explore the repo directly.

  HOW TO WORK — exactly as you would in a productive Claude Code or
  Codex session handed the one-line brief "align the documentation
  with the état de l'art of the repo." Sequence it however you like —
  survey broadly then fix, or align as you go, or anything between; no
  phase is mandated and you decide what to align. Two things worth
  stating up front, the rest is your judgment:
    - The advisory hints are a cheap starting point, NOT your scope.
      Don't stop when they're done — most real drift is SEMANTIC (wrong
      defaults, outdated behaviour, stale examples, features shipped
      weeks ago and never documented) and invisible to any scan. Read
      the code and its recent evolution (`git log`,
      `recently_changed_code_files`) and align what you find.
    - When a doc is slightly AHEAD of the code (a documented ambition
      the code has not caught up with), do NOT align it down to the
      current behaviour — that is the UNFULFILLED PROMISE path
      (outcome c below).
    - COVER THE WHOLE CORPUS, not just the obvious docs. The long tail
      is where drift hides: every `bots/*/README.md` against its
      `main.bot`, the cloud/architecture/troubleshooting docs against
      the real config values, the studio docs. On a corpus this large
      you have the subagent tool — fan out sub-auditors by cluster
      (bots, cloud, studio, references…) to read each area against its
      code in parallel, then align what they surface, exactly as you
      would orchestrate a big native session. A single sweep that
      stays on the headline docs is the shallow failure to avoid.

  For each misalignment you act on, READ the code that grounds it (the
  tool invocation — read_file / grep / a --help run — is your evidence;
  a fix whose rationale cannot cite it is a façade), then adjudicate to
  exactly ONE of FOUR outcomes:
       a. FIX + COMMIT — the doc is stale (or the doc is missing):
          make the claim fully correct and complete — bring the doc up
          to the état de l'art of the code, not the most minimal token
          change that mutes the scanner (but no padding, no invented
          prose) — or write the missing documentation, code-grounded,
          placed
          per the `doc-enrichment` skill (an existing doc first; a
          new `.md` under {{vars.docs_dir}} only when none fits) →
          run the negative-space check (`git grep` for stale
          cross-references to whatever you renamed/removed, fix
          those too) → COMMIT the aligned doc with a semantic
          message (`docs(<area>): <what was aligned>`), body ending
          with the trailer line `Bot: docs-refresh`. Use
          `fix(<area>):` ONLY for a demonstrably misleading code
          COMMENT (go_comment_globs opt-in). Stage everything
          including new files (`git add -A`) before each commit.
       b. DISMISS + LEDGER — a false positive, or genuinely not worth
          documenting: record it in the DISMISSALS LEDGER (below)
          with a reason. Dismissing without recording is what makes
          the same non-issue come back to you next pass.
       c. PROMISE + PROMISES LEDGER — the claim is a deliberate,
          still-wanted ambition the code has not caught up with: see
          UNFULFILLED PROMISES below. Never delete it, never "align"
          it down to the current code.
       d. CODE BUG + BOARD — the doc is right and the code is broken:
          the is_code_bug escape valve (rule 5).

  COMMIT LOCALLY ONLY — never `git push`, never open a PR or MR
  yourself. Publication is owned by the run's finalize tail
  (deterministic gates + one canonical PR); a campaign that pushes
  creates a duplicate PR next to the tail's (observed live: run
  019f8bb4 produced #278 AND #279 for the same three commits). Your
  commits on the local branch ARE your delivery.

  3. COMMIT EACH DOC AS YOU ALIGN IT — never batch. git IS your
     durable state: an interrupted run keeps every doc you committed,
     and a fresh pass just reads `git log` + a fresh advisory report
     and continues. Do NOT keep a separate worklist file.

  4. WRITEABLE SET — `.md` files, plus (ONLY when `go_comment_globs`
     is non-empty) comment-only edits in Go files matching those
     globs. NEVER touch code statements, configs, or build files. A
     deterministic scope gate diffs the whole run against its base
     after every pass: any out-of-scope file FAILS the gate and
     bounces back to you — you would then have to revert those
     commits before continuing. Don't make it fire.

  5. ESCAPE VALVE — is_code_bug. When a doc is CORRECT and the CODE
     is wrong, do NOT "align" the doc to broken behaviour. Set
     is_code_bug=true in your termination output, name the case in
     human_note, and (for a genuinely blocking ambiguity) pause via
     the ask-user tool. Filing it as a board finding (see below) is
     the default; pausing is for when you cannot proceed without the
     answer. Distinct from an UNFULFILLED PROMISE (outcome c): there
     the code is not buggy, it is merely behind a documented ambition.

  6. BASELINE — don't chase failures you didn't cause. A build/test
     failure that predates this run (see `baseline`, or establish it
     once cheaply with a `git stash`-based check) is out of scope:
     note it and move on. Docs-only passes rarely trip the build
     gate; comment edits can.

  7. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. Keep operator pauses RARE — most
     adjudications are yours to make; lower-stakes doubts go in
     `summary`.

  FINDINGS HANDOFF — after your last commit, capture anything OUT OF
  SCOPE but worth tracking (a code bug found while verifying a claim,
  an undocumented capability worth its own ticket, an infrastructure
  issue): call `mcp__iterion_board__list_issues` with
  `{ "state": "inbox" }` first to avoid duplicates, then
  `mcp__iterion_board__create_issue` per NEW finding with state
  "inbox" and labels ["findings", "kind:<bug|drift|improvement|
  question>", "source:docs_refresh", "area:<subsystem>",
  "severity:<low|medium|high>"]. Zero findings is a fine outcome. If
  the board tools are unavailable, list the findings in `summary`.

  DISMISSALS LEDGER — the persistence half of adjudication. For EVERY
  hint or self-found candidate you judge not-worth-acting-on, append
  one entry to the JSON array at `{{vars.dismissed_path}}` (create the
  file — `[]` — and its parent directory on first use;
  read-modify-write, keep it valid JSON):
    {"doc": "<doc, or empty for a repo-wide item>",
     "kind": "<hint kind, or your own>", "value": "<the value>",
     "reason": "<one line>"}
  The scan excludes ledger entries from every later pass, so a
  settled adjudication never re-surfaces. A dismissal that is not
  recorded WILL come back to you next pass — recording it is part of
  adjudicating it, exactly like committing is part of fixing.

  UNFULFILLED PROMISES — adjudication outcome (c). Some claims that
  fail verification are not stale prose: they read as a DELIBERATE
  ambition (an announced feature, a target contract the code has not
  caught up with — roadmap phrasing, "will/planned", an accepted ADR
  not yet implemented). Do NOT delete such a claim and do NOT "align"
  it down to the current code. Instead:
    - Append one entry to the JSON array at
      `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/promises.json` (create the file — `[]` —
      and its parent directory on first use; read-modify-write, keep
      it valid JSON; passes are fresh sessions, so the FILE is the
      only way the list survives to the PR tail):
        {"doc": "<doc path>", "claim": "<the promised behaviour>",
         "code_gap": "<big picture: what the code lacks>",
         "note": "<one line>"}
    - ALSO record the item in the DISMISSALS LEDGER (reason:
      "promise: <short ref>") so it stops re-surfacing.
    - OPTIONALLY (your judgment, case by case) add a clearly-flagged,
      honest status note in the doc itself ("Implementation status:
      …; remaining work: …") and commit it — documenting the gap IS a
      legitimate doc↔reality alignment.
  Promises never block convergence — they are reported, not gated:
  the PR tail lists them under an "Unfulfilled documented promises"
  section.

  KEEP GOING — align the real drift you find, across the corpus, not
  just the handful of scanned hints. A pass that touches only the
  hinted docs and declares done is the exact failure this bot exists to
  prevent. Stop this pass when a fresh look would find little more to
  align (that "little more" IS your docs_aligned signal), or when you
  reach the edge of your budget — then report. There is no prize for a
  small diff, and none for padding: report what is true.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the
  engine detects completion; an under-specified end just gets
  re-poked):
    - docs_aligned: the ASYMPTOTE signal. true ONLY when THIS pass
      converged the corpus — a fresh comprehensive survey would find
      little or nothing left to align (no remaining real drift, no
      significant missing docs). If this pass produced MANY alignments,
      assume the next comprehensive sweep will still find real work and
      report false: the run loops you back on a fresh tree, and it is
      the DECLINE of alignments-per-pass toward zero that ends the run
      — exactly like re-running a Claude Code plan until the plan comes
      back empty. Report true only when you have genuinely reached that
      floor (everything surfaced is committed, dismissed, or a promise).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - drift_remaining: a concise note of what still needs work (empty
      when docs_aligned).
    - is_code_bug: true when you found ≥1 place where the doc is
      right and the code is wrong (detail in human_note / the board
      finding).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator
      decision.
    - summary: which docs you aligned or wrote (with the verification
      evidence pattern you used), what you dismissed and why.

  Be honest. A deterministic scope gate and a build gate re-check the
  tree after you, and the run loops you back until you report
  docs_aligned AND the gates are green — so under-reporting only
  costs a pass, and over-reporting lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Scope notes (operator's attention pin, may be empty):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  ## Advisory scan report (this pass)
  {{input.hints_note}}

  Hints — ADVISORY, sorted by signal; each entry:
  {doc, line, kind, value, note}. Use them as a starting point,
  contradict them freely (dismiss to the ledger with a reason), and
  explore BEYOND them — the scan sees paths and links, you see
  meaning:
  {{input.hints}}

  Recently changed code files (prioritisation hint, may be empty):
  {{input.recently_changed_code_files}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures to SKIP (empty = establish once
  cheaply if the build gate goes red):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the
  first pass; when present it is EITHER a scope violation — files
  outside the doc writeable-set changed; revert those commits before
  continuing — OR a real build/test failure to fix):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  Plan the whole corpus first, then align it — for each item: verify
  in the code → bring the doc fully up to the code (repair stale, or
  write the missing doc) → negative-space check → commit (`docs(<area>):
  …` + `Bot: docs-refresh` trailer). Work through the entire plan this
  pass, to the edge of your budget, then report the termination
  contract (docs_aligned as the asymptote signal, commits_this_pass,
  drift_remaining). Read `git log` first to see what earlier passes
  already aligned.

## ─── Prompts : Default-create (author docs from zero) ──────────────

prompt author_docs_system:
  You are the doc author for the `docs-refresh` workflow, invoked on a
  repository that currently has NO documentation in scope. Your job is
  to BOOTSTRAP an initial documentation set by reading the code and
  writing accurate Markdown, so the run's normal alignment campaign has
  something real to polish.

  Load the `docs-refresh.md` skill for the operating context.

  WHAT TO CREATE:
   - Create the docs directory (`{{input.docs_dir}}`) if it does not
     exist, and author your documentation there as `.md` files.
   - Author or refresh a top-level `README.md` if the repo has none — a
     short overview that links into `{{input.docs_dir}}/`.
   - Cover, at minimum: a project overview (what it is / what it does),
     how to build & run it, the high-level architecture or module map,
     and how to use the main entry points. Add further pages only where
     the code clearly warrants them — do not pad.

  HARD CONSTRAINTS:
   1. Documentation only — write `.md` files. NEVER modify source code,
      build files, or configuration.
   2. EVIDENCE OVER INVENTION. Every statement must reflect what the
      code actually does. Read the relevant files before describing
      them; never document aspirational or assumed behaviour. If you
      cannot verify a claim, omit it rather than guess.
   3. STACK-AGNOSTIC. This repo may be in ANY language. Detect the
      language / build system from the repo itself (manifest files,
      entry points, lockfiles) — never assume a particular ecosystem.
   4. Do NOT commit. A later step commits on convergence.

prompt author_docs_user:
  Author initial documentation for the repository at
  {{input.workspace_dir}}.

  Target docs directory (create if absent): {{input.docs_dir}}

  Recently changed code files (a hint, may be empty — non-authoritative):
  {{input.recently_changed_code_files}}

  Steps:
   1. Survey the repository to understand its purpose, language/stack,
      structure, and entry points (read manifest files, top-level
      directories, and the main entry file(s)).
   2. Create `{{input.docs_dir}}/` if needed and author the initial
      `.md` pages described in your system prompt, each grounded in what
      you read. Author/refresh a root `README.md` overview if missing.
   3. Keep each page focused and accurate; cross-link the pages.

  Output fields:
   - docs_created: true if you created at least one doc file
   - created_doc_files: repo-relative paths you wrote
   - summary: what you authored, and the evidence (files you read)
     behind the key claims

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a
## DETERMINISTIC tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit
## code. Docs-only passes should always be green here; the gate earns its
## keep when go_comment_globs opts comment edits in (the tree must still
## compile) and as the universal engine-side floor shared with the other v2
## bots. verify_build does NOT fix anything: a RED gate routes back to
## `campaign`.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A doc-alignment pass just committed changes
  to this repository (documentation, possibly code COMMENTS). Your single
  job: capture how THIS repo builds and tests itself into a re-runnable
  script the deterministic gate will execute. You do NOT change anything.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain, HONOURING any pinned toolchain
     (version managers, lockfiles, devbox, GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a
     language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build (and the
     fast test targets a comment-only change could plausibly affect) and
     exits non-zero on any failure. For a docs-only repo or a pass that
     touched only `.md` files, a build-only script (or the closest cheap
     sanity command) is appropriate — keep it FAST.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: scope `verify.sh` so a red base tree can't
     absorb the run; list any excluded pre-existing failures in your
     summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid. You do NOT fix
     anything here.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config),
     write a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its
  real exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  exists; summary = the command you settled on.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build (+ fast tests where comment edits could
  plausibly matter) into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` (out-of-tree —
  see the system prompt + the `verify-build` skill). Do NOT change
  anything; the deterministic gate runs your script and the campaign
  fixes any red on the next pass.

  MANDATORY (skill section 1b): if the repo's own CI enforces a
  codegen-drift gate (git diff --exit-code after a regen/vendor step,
  a drift-check task, test -z on git status --porcelain), verify.sh
  MUST mirror it — run the regen command(s), then fail on drift; the
  deterministic precheck rejects a gateless verify.sh otherwise.

## ─── PR finalization prompts (opt-in) ──────────────────────────────

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a finished docs-refresh run by opening ONE pull request
  (PR; merge request on GitLab) for the SERIES of per-doc alignment
  commits this run produced, then (optionally) posting the PR link back
  to the source issue. You make NO further changes to docs or code.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and
  open the PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of
       the base (`git rev-list --count origin/<base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do
       NOT open an empty PR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/docs-refresh/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set,
       else the repo's default branch — see the skill).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token
       file when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is
       available, set opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never
       pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the PR
       from that branch into the base. Title: a concise
       "docs: …" conventional summary. Body: the campaign summary, the
       docs aligned this run, and — when drift_remaining is non-empty —
       an honest "Remaining drift" section (the run shipped what is
       banked; never present a partial alignment as complete).
       PROMISES: read the promises ledger file named in your user
       prompt (it may be absent or empty — then skip this). When it
       has entries, append an "Unfulfilled documented promises"
       section to the PR body: one bullet per entry — the doc, the
       promised claim, and the big-picture code gap. These are
       deliberate documented ambitions the code has not caught up
       with, recorded by the campaign for the maintainers; report
       them verbatim in substance, never silently drop them.
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened PR URL back to it: a
       forge issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>"
       ref → use the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short
  summary. Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → repo default branch): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Operator scope notes (may be empty): {{input.scope_notes}}

  Campaign summary (for the PR body):
  {{input.campaign_summary}}

  Remaining drift reported by the campaign (empty = fully aligned):
  {{input.drift_remaining}}

  Promises ledger (JSON array; the file may be absent or empty):
  {{vars.scratch_dir}}/promises.json

  Push the branch, open the PR (with the "Unfulfilled documented
  promises" section when the ledger has entries), post the back-link
  when a source issue is given, and return the structured result.

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## scan_hints output — the ADVISORY report + routing booleans. hints
## is a bounded JSON array of {doc, line, kind, value, note} entries
## (kinds: missing_path, dead_link, dead_anchor, unmentioned_area);
## every *_count field is TELEMETRY (report/events), never a gate
## condition. noop_skip / no_docs drive the two special routes.
schema hints_output:
  doc_files: string[]
  doc_count: int
  no_docs: bool
  noop_skip: bool
  noop_reason: string
  git_head: string
  hints: json
  hint_count: int
  missing_path_count: int
  dead_link_count: int
  unmentioned_area_count: int
  checked_paths: int
  checked_links: int
  ledger_excluded: int
  truncated: bool
  hints_note: string
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]

## campaign input: the advisory report + the deterministic gate
## feedback from the previous pass (empty on the first pass and on a
## green-but-more-work continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  hints: json
  hint_count: int
  hints_note: string
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]
  fail_log: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output
## the engine keys convergence on. docs_aligned is the done-oracle;
## the gate ANDs it with the scope check and the build gate — nothing
## else.
schema campaign_output:
  docs_aligned: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  drift_remaining: string
  is_code_bug: bool
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC build gate's contract. `passed` is
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT
## an LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was
## produced (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## verify_precheck: deterministic "did anything change since the last
## GREEN verify?" — HEAD equality + clean tree (or a docs-only diff)
## ⇔ the previous verdict still holds and the whole verify_build (LLM
## agent) + verify_run (real build+tests) chain is skipped. Pass 1
## always verifies (no state file yet); a RED verdict is never reused.
schema verify_precheck_output:
  reuse: bool
  reason: string

## scope_check is the DETERMINISTIC writeable-set gate: every path
## changed since the RUN BASE must be a doc (`.md`), the audit cache
## file, or (when go_comment_globs is non-empty) a file inside those
## globs. Out-of-scope changes fail the gate and bounce back to the
## campaign, which must revert them.
schema scope_check_output:
  scope_ok: bool
  out_of_scope: string[]
  log: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = build
## green AND scope clean AND the campaign reports the docs aligned —
## NOTHING ELSE (v3: scanner counts are telemetry, not conditions).
## fail_log relays the scope violation (priority) or the real build
## failure to the campaign on a red pass; empty when green.
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

## ─── PR finalization schemas (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output field so the two
## outgoing edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool

## forge_auth_probe output — deterministic "can we push?" so finalize_mr (an
## LLM agent) is only ever entered when a push credential actually exists.
schema forge_auth_state:
  available: bool
  reason: string

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string
  campaign_summary: string
  drift_remaining: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string

schema author_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  docs_dir: string
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]

schema author_output:
  docs_created: bool
  created_doc_files: string[]
  summary: string

## mark_issue_for_review's free-form output: JSON-as-string on success
## or {"skipped":"no issue_id"} on the manual-CLI no-op path.
schema mark_issue_output:
  text: string

## update_audit_cache (v3 — noop cache only): records the git HEAD the
## docs were aligned to; the NEXT run's scan_hints compares it against
## the live HEAD for the noop short-circuit.
schema update_audit_cache_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  cache_path: string

schema update_audit_cache_output:
  cache_path: string
  git_head: string
  skipped: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## scan_hints — the ONE deterministic producer of ADVISORY hints (v3:
## replaces scan_docs + scan_code_surface + build_manifest). Runs at
## entry and as the continuation-loop head, so the report always
## reflects the live tree and hints the campaign resolved (fixed docs,
## ledger entries) retire between passes.
##
## What it checks (high-precision, universal by construction):
##   - missing_path  — a path cited in a doc that is gone from disk
##                     AND that git once tracked (index ∪ deletion
##                     history). Two precision boundaries: the
##                     first-segment rule skips foreign-tool example
##                     lines (docker / gh, other repos' layouts — the
##                     v2.5 foreign-flag class, ~200 FPs on one live
##                     run, is structurally absent because CLI flags
##                     are simply not scanned); the git-tracked rule
##                     skips example/placeholder paths under real top
##                     dirs and runtime-only files (bots/my-bot/…,
##                     .claude/settings.json — 604 "missing" / 43%
##                     noise on run 019f8ba3 without it). A path git
##                     never knew is not doc drift.
##   - dead_link /   — internal markdown links whose target file or
##     dead_anchor     GitHub-slug heading anchor does not resolve.
##   - unmentioned_area — top-level dirs (+ children of generic
##                     src-style container roots) no doc mentions.
##                     Advisory ZONES, not obligations — "internal,
##                     not user-facing" is a legitimate dismissal.
## Ledger entries (vars.dismissed_path) are excluded — the campaign's
## settled adjudications never re-surface. When nothing is derivable
## (no docs, or docs citing nothing checkable) the producer degrades
## SILENTLY: empty hints + an explicit "explore directly" note, exit 0.
##
## Also owns the two special routes: noop_skip (cache git_head ==
## HEAD, clean tree, no explicit request → done, zero LLM cost) and
## no_docs (→ author_docs DEFAULT-CREATE).
##
## Raw-string command — the python body avoids double quotes and
## literal backticks (BT = chr(96)); values flow via shell env vars.
tool scan_hints:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} DOC_GLOBS={{vars.doc_globs}} EXCLUDED_DIRS={{vars.excluded_dirs}} BUNDLE_SELF={{vars.bundle_self_path}} DIFF_SINCE={{vars.diff_since}} CACHE_PATH={{vars.audit_cache_path}} ISSUE_ID={{vars.issue_id}} DISMISSED_PATH={{vars.dismissed_path}} MAX_HINTS={{vars.max_hints}} DOCS_DIR={{vars.docs_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, json, glob, re, subprocess
ws = os.environ['WS']
os.chdir(ws)
globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('DOC_GLOBS', '').split(',') if g.strip()]
EXCLUDE_DIRS = {d.strip() for d in os.environ.get('EXCLUDED_DIRS', '').split(',') if d.strip()}
bundle_self = os.environ.get('BUNDLE_SELF', '').strip().rstrip(os.sep)
docs_dir = os.environ.get('DOCS_DIR', '').strip().rstrip(os.sep)
try:
    max_hints = int(os.environ.get('MAX_HINTS', '120') or '120')
except ValueError:
    max_hints = 120
def excluded(p):
    if any(part in EXCLUDE_DIRS for part in p.split(os.sep)):
        return True
    if bundle_self and (p == bundle_self or p.startswith(bundle_self + os.sep)):
        return True
    return False
files = sorted({f for g in globs for f in glob.glob(g, recursive=True) if os.path.isfile(f) and not excluded(f)})
no_docs = len(files) == 0
def git_head():
    try:
        return subprocess.run(['git', 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        return ''
cache_path = os.environ.get('CACHE_PATH', '').strip()
cache_abs = os.path.abspath(cache_path) if cache_path else ''
def worktree_dirty():
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['git', 'status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=all'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
    except Exception:
        return True
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        path = line[3:].strip() if len(line) > 3 else ''
        if not path:
            continue
        if cache_abs and os.path.abspath(path) == cache_abs:
            continue
        if excluded(path):
            continue
        return True
    return False
cache_head = ''
if cache_path:
    try:
        with open(cache_path, encoding='utf-8') as cf:
            cache_head = (json.load(cf).get('git_head', '') or '')
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError, AttributeError):
        pass
issue_id = os.environ.get('ISSUE_ID', '').strip()
head = git_head()
noop_skip = False
if no_docs:
    noop_reason = 'no docs in scope'
elif issue_id:
    noop_reason = 'explicit request (issue_id set)'
elif not head:
    noop_reason = 'HEAD unavailable (not a git repo?)'
elif not cache_head:
    noop_reason = 'no cached git_head (first aligned run)'
elif head != cache_head:
    noop_reason = 'HEAD advanced since last alignment'
elif worktree_dirty():
    noop_reason = 'worktree has uncommitted changes'
else:
    noop_skip = True
    noop_reason = 'docs already aligned to HEAD ' + head[:12]
dismissed = set()
dismissed_path = os.environ.get('DISMISSED_PATH', '').strip()
if dismissed_path and os.path.exists(dismissed_path):
    try:
        with open(dismissed_path, encoding='utf-8') as df:
            for e in json.load(df):
                if isinstance(e, dict):
                    dismissed.add((e.get('doc', ''), e.get('kind', ''), str(e.get('value', ''))))
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError):
        pass
hints = []
ledger_excluded = 0
def add_hint(doc, line, kind, value, note):
    global ledger_excluded
    if (doc, kind, str(value)) in dismissed:
        ledger_excluded += 1
        return
    hints.append({'doc': doc, 'line': line, 'kind': kind, 'value': value, 'note': note})
BT = chr(96)
MD_LINK_RE = re.compile(r'\[(?:[^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)')
PATH_RE = re.compile(r'(?<![\w./-])((?:[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/)+[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\.[A-Za-z0-9]{1,8})(?![\w/-])')
_heading_cache = {}
def slugify(text):
    s = text.strip().lower().replace(BT, '')
    s = re.sub(r'[^\w\s-]', '', s)
    s = re.sub(r'\s', '-', s)
    return s.strip('-')
def headings_of(path):
    if path in _heading_cache:
        return _heading_cache[path]
    slugs = set()
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh:
            for ln in fh:
                hm = re.match(r'\s{0,3}(#{1,6})\s+(.+)', ln)
                if hm:
                    slugs.add(slugify(hm.group(2)))
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        pass
    _heading_cache[path] = slugs
    return slugs
## A cited path that is missing on disk is a DRIFT signal only if git
## ever TRACKED it (current index or a path deleted somewhere in
## history): docs constantly cite example/placeholder paths under real
## top dirs (bots/my-bot/main.bot) and runtime-only files
## (.claude/settings.json) — never tracked, never drift. Observed live
## (run 019f8ba3): the existence-only rule flagged 604 missing paths,
## ~43% of everything checked. On a shallow clone the history half is
## partial and the check degrades toward SILENCE (fewer hints), never
## toward noise; with no git at all the set stays empty and the plain
## existence rule applies (fixtures/tests).
ever_tracked = set()
try:
    _ls = subprocess.run(['git', 'ls-files'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
    if _ls.returncode == 0:
        ever_tracked.update(p for p in _ls.stdout.splitlines() if p)
    _dl = subprocess.run(['git', 'log', '--pretty=format:', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=D'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=120)
    if _dl.returncode == 0:
        ever_tracked.update(p for p in _dl.stdout.splitlines() if p)
except Exception:
    ever_tracked = set()
checked_paths = 0
checked_links = 0
corpus_parts = []
for doc in files:
    try:
        with open(doc, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh:
            lines = fh.readlines()
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        continue
    corpus_parts.append(''.join(lines))
    seen = set()
    for lineno, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        for m in MD_LINK_RE.finditer(line):
            target = m.group(1)
            if target.startswith('#'):
                continue
            if re.match(r'^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://', target) or target.startswith(('mailto:', 'tel:', '//', 'www.')):
                continue
            if '#' in target:
                path_part, frag = target.split('#', 1)
            else:
                path_part, frag = target, ''
            key = ('link', target)
            if key in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(key)
            checked_links += 1
            tgt = doc if not path_part else os.path.normpath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(doc), path_part))
            if path_part and not os.path.exists(tgt):
                add_hint(doc, lineno, 'dead_link', target, 'link target not found: ' + tgt)
            elif frag and not re.match(r'^[Ll][0-9]', frag) and tgt.endswith('.md') and frag.lower() not in headings_of(tgt):
                add_hint(doc, lineno, 'dead_anchor', target, 'no heading matching #' + frag + ' in ' + tgt)
        for m in PATH_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            root = value.split('/', 1)[0]
            ## Precision boundary. With git available, ever_tracked IS the
            ## boundary: a missing path hints iff git once tracked it —
            ## foreign-tool examples, placeholders and runtime files are
            ## silently skipped, and a fully-removed directory still
            ## surfaces its tracked files (the first-segment rule would
            ## mask that drift). Without git (fixtures), fall back to the
            ## first-segment-exists-as-repo-directory rule.
            if not ever_tracked and not os.path.isdir(root):
                continue
            key = ('path', value)
            if key in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(key)
            if ever_tracked and not os.path.exists(value) and value not in ever_tracked:
                continue
            checked_paths += 1
            if not os.path.exists(value):
                add_hint(doc, lineno, 'missing_path', value, 'path cited in the doc not found on disk (git tracked it before)' if ever_tracked else 'path cited in the doc not found on disk (' + root + '/ exists)')
corpus = chr(10).join(corpus_parts)
SRC_ROOTS = {'src', 'lib', 'pkg', 'cmd', 'app', 'apps', 'packages', 'internal', 'modules', 'crates', 'services', 'components'}
def significant(d):
    for root, dirs, fs in os.walk(d):
        dirs[:] = [x for x in dirs if not x.startswith('.') and x not in EXCLUDE_DIRS]
        if any(not x.startswith('.') for x in fs):
            return True
    return False
areas = []
try:
    top_entries = sorted(os.listdir('.'))
except OSError:
    top_entries = []
for name in top_entries:
    if name.startswith('.') or name in EXCLUDE_DIRS or name == docs_dir:
        continue
    if not os.path.isdir(name) or excluded(name):
        continue
    if name in SRC_ROOTS:
        try:
            subs = sorted(os.listdir(name))
        except OSError:
            subs = []
        for sub in subs:
            p = os.path.join(name, sub)
            if sub.startswith('.') or sub in EXCLUDE_DIRS or not os.path.isdir(p) or excluded(p):
                continue
            if significant(p):
                areas.append(p)
    elif significant(name):
        areas.append(name)
for area in areas:
    if os.sep in area:
        mentioned = area in corpus
    else:
        mentioned = re.search(r'(?<![\w-])' + re.escape(area) + r'(?![\w-])', corpus, re.IGNORECASE) is not None
    if not mentioned:
        add_hint('', 0, 'unmentioned_area', area, 'code area ' + area + os.sep + ' appears in no doc in scope (advisory zone — may be internal)')
prio = {'missing_path': 0, 'dead_link': 0, 'dead_anchor': 0, 'unmentioned_area': 1}
hints.sort(key=lambda h: (prio.get(h['kind'], 2), h['doc'], h['line']))
missing_path_count = sum(1 for h in hints if h['kind'] == 'missing_path')
dead_link_count = sum(1 for h in hints if h['kind'] in ('dead_link', 'dead_anchor'))
unmentioned_area_count = sum(1 for h in hints if h['kind'] == 'unmentioned_area')
truncated = max_hints > 0 and len(hints) > max_hints
if max_hints > 0:
    hints = hints[:max_hints]
if no_docs:
    hints_note = 'no docs in scope'
elif checked_paths + checked_links == 0 and not areas:
    hints_note = 'no mechanical signal derivable from these docs (no repo-rooted paths or internal links cited) — the scan degrades silently; explore the repo directly'
else:
    hints_note = str(missing_path_count) + ' missing path(s), ' + str(dead_link_count) + ' dead link(s)/anchor(s), ' + str(unmentioned_area_count) + ' unmentioned area(s) — from ' + str(checked_paths) + ' checkable path(s) + ' + str(checked_links) + ' internal link(s) across ' + str(len(files)) + ' doc(s); ' + str(ledger_excluded) + ' ledger-dismissed excluded' + (' [list truncated to ' + str(max_hints) + ']' if truncated else '')
recent = []
diff_since = os.environ.get('DIFF_SINCE', '').strip()
if diff_since:
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['git', 'diff', '--name-only', diff_since + '...HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
        recent = sorted([l for l in proc.stdout.splitlines() if l and not excluded(l)])
    except Exception:
        recent = []
print(json.dumps({'doc_files': files, 'doc_count': len(files), 'no_docs': no_docs, 'noop_skip': noop_skip, 'noop_reason': noop_reason, 'git_head': head, 'hints': hints, 'hint_count': len(hints), 'missing_path_count': missing_path_count, 'dead_link_count': dead_link_count, 'unmentioned_area_count': unmentioned_area_count, 'checked_paths': checked_paths, 'checked_links': checked_links, 'ledger_excluded': ledger_excluded, 'truncated': truncated, 'hints_note': hints_note, 'recently_changed_code_files': recent}))
"`
  output: hints_output

agent author_docs:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-max}"
  input: author_input
  output: author_output
  system: author_docs_system
  user: author_docs_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset so it works exactly like a native session: survey docs +
## code (the advisory report is a starting point, never the scope),
## verify in the code, fix or write the doc, commit each aligned doc
## in stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass re-derives its todo from a
## fresh advisory report + `git log` (git is the durable state). Board
## capabilities power the findings handoff (code bugs found while
## verifying claims → inbox).
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  ## ultracode (on opus-4-8) hands the campaign the `agent` subagent
  ## tool + the orchestration prerogative — so it can fan out its own
  ## sub-auditors across the corpus, EXACTLY as a native Claude Code
  ## session does. A single agent (any prompt) self-scopes to the
  ## obvious docs and misses the long tail; a 3-way live benchmark
  ## (2026-07-23) showed the only run that reached the full corpus —
  ## bot READMEs + cloud + studio — was the one that DECOMPOSED. This
  ## is coverage via the agent's own judgment, not engine fan-out.
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-ultracode}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human
  capabilities: [board.read, board.create]

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). Kept FAST for docs-only passes.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-medium}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks
## (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} RUN_ID={{run.id}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def tree_state():
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
    except Exception:
        return None
pre = tree_state()
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
# Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
# deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
# CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
# must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
if rc == 0:
    import glob
    def has_drift_gate(text):
        for l in text.splitlines():
            # git diff --exit-code is the canonical build-failing gate.
            if 'diff' in l and '--exit-code' in l:
                return True
            # --quiet / status --porcelain also appear as commit-if-changed
            # control flow in updater workflows (e.g. a Homebrew tap bump) --
            # only count them when the same line actually fails the build.
            gated = ('exit 1' in l) or ('|| exit' in l) or ('test -z' in l) or ('[ -z' in l)
            if gated and (('diff' in l and '--quiet' in l) or 'status --porcelain' in l):
                return True
        return False
    ci_hit = ''
    for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
        for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
            try:
                if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                    ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                    break
            except OSError:
                pass
        if ci_hit:
            break
    if ci_hit:
        try:
            vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
        except OSError:
            vs = ''
        if not has_drift_gate(vs):
            rc = 3
            out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
    if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
        post = tree_state()
        if post is not None:
            fresh = sorted(post - pre)
            if fresh:
                rc = 4
                out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
## Record the verified HEAD so verify_precheck can reuse a GREEN
## verdict on a later pass OF THIS RUN. Stamped with the run id:
## scratch is host-persistent across local runs, and reusing a
## PREVIOUS run's verdict would let a fresh run reach the gate with
## outputs.verify_run never resolved — it could then never converge.
if rc == 0:
    try:
        head = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=30).stdout.strip()
        with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.state'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as sf:
            json.dump({'head': head, 'rc': rc, 'run_id': os.environ.get('RUN_ID', '')}, sf)
    except Exception:
        pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## scope_check — the DETERMINISTIC writeable-set gate. Diffs the RUN
## BASE (oldest reflog entry of the fresh per-run worktree — exact
## under worktree: auto) against the working tree and flags any changed
## path that is not a doc: `.md` anywhere, the audit cache file, or
## (when GO_GLOBS is non-empty) a file matching those globs (the
## comment-only discipline stays contract-level — this gate is
## path-level containment). Untracked files are held to the same rule.
## No LLM; the campaign must revert whatever this flags.
tool scope_check:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} GO_GLOBS={{vars.go_comment_globs}} CACHE={{vars.audit_cache_path}} python3 -c "
import os, json, subprocess, fnmatch
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
go_globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('GO_GLOBS', '').split(',') if g.strip()]
cache = os.environ.get('CACHE', '').strip()

def git(args):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws] + args, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=120)
        return p.stdout or ''
    except Exception:
        return ''

reflog = [l for l in git(['reflog', '--format=%H']).splitlines() if l.strip()]
base = reflog[-1] if reflog else 'HEAD'
changed = set(l for l in git(['diff', base, '--name-only']).splitlines() if l.strip())
for line in git(['status', '--porcelain']).splitlines():
    if line[:2].strip() == '??':
        changed.add(line[3:].strip())

def allowed(p):
    if p.endswith('.md'):
        return True
    if cache and p == cache:
        return True
    for g in go_globs:
        if fnmatch.fnmatch(p, g):
            return True
    return False

out_of_scope = sorted(p for p in changed if not allowed(p))
log = ''
if out_of_scope:
    log = 'SCOPE VIOLATION: this run changed files outside the doc writeable-set (.md' + (' + go_comment_globs' if go_globs else '') + '). Revert these before continuing (git revert the offending commits, or git checkout ' + base[:12] + ' -- <path>): ' + ', '.join(out_of_scope[:40])
print(json.dumps({'scope_ok': not out_of_scope, 'out_of_scope': out_of_scope[:100], 'log': log}))
"`
  output: scope_check_output

## verify_precheck — the deterministic fast path around the verify
## chain. Reuses the last GREEN verdict when HEAD is unchanged and the
## tree is clean, AND (docs-only fast path) when every path changed
## since the green HEAD is a `.md` file — a doc edit is invisible to
## the repo's build (observed live: verify_build+verify_run burned
## 5-16 min PER PASS re-verifying docs-only commits — ~half the run's
## wall-clock, run 019f8b50). Anything else (first pass, code-comment
## edits, dirty tree, red previous verdict) re-verifies.
tool verify_precheck:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} RUN_ID={{run.id}} python3 -c "
import os, json, subprocess
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
state_path = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.state')
def out(reuse, reason):
    print(json.dumps({'reuse': reuse, 'reason': reason}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
if not os.path.exists(state_path):
    out(False, 'no previous green verify (first pass)')
try:
    with open(state_path, encoding='utf-8') as sf:
        state = json.load(sf)
except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
    out(False, 'verify.state unreadable')
if state.get('rc') != 0:
    out(False, 'previous verify was not green')
## Reuse is RUN-SCOPED: scratch persists across local runs, and a
## previous run's verdict must never satisfy a run in which verify_run
## has not executed (outputs.verify_run would be unresolved at the
## gate and the run could never converge).
if state.get('run_id', '') != os.environ.get('RUN_ID', ''):
    out(False, 'verify.state belongs to another run')
try:
    head = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=30).stdout.strip()
    dirty = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60).stdout.strip()
except Exception as e:
    out(False, 'git state unreadable: ' + str(e))
if head and head == state.get('head') and not dirty:
    out(True, 'HEAD ' + head[:12] + ' unchanged since last green verify, tree clean')
## Docs-only fast-path: commits landed since the green verify, but if
## EVERY changed path is a markdown doc the build/test verdict cannot
## have changed — a .md edit is invisible to the repo's build. This is
## a deterministic file-extension rule, not an LLM judgment; anything
## else (code-comment fixes via go_comment_globs, cache files, unknown
## extensions) still triggers the full verify chain.
green = state.get('head', '')
if head and green and not dirty:
    try:
        diff = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'diff', '--name-only', green, head], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        changed = [p for p in diff.stdout.splitlines() if p.strip()]
    except Exception:
        changed = None
    if changed and diff.returncode == 0 and all(p.lower().endswith('.md') for p in changed):
        out(True, 'only markdown docs changed since green verify ' + green[:12] + ' (' + str(len(changed)) + ' file(s)), tree clean — build verdict unchanged')
out(False, 'tree changed since last green verify')
"`
  output: verify_precheck_output

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. converged = build
## green AND scope clean AND the campaign reports the docs aligned —
## NOTHING ELSE (v3). The advisory hint counts are telemetry in the
## run events/report, never conditions: the campaign's honest
## termination contract + the two truth gates are the whole oracle.
## fail_log priority: scope violation (the campaign must revert) >
## build failure > empty (green-but-more-work → the fresh advisory
## report is the guidance). No LLM, no shell. On a verify_precheck
## reuse pass, outputs.verify_run still resolves to the last REAL
## execution — the verdict being reused.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.scope_check.scope_ok && outputs.campaign.docs_aligned"
    fail_log: "if(!outputs.scope_check.scope_ok, outputs.scope_check.log, if(outputs.verify_run.passed, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail))"

## mark_issue_for_review: after the run reaches a clean terminal
## state, transition the tracker issue out of `ready` so the
## dispatcher's polling loop doesn't re-dispatch it on the next tick.
## Skipped (no-op exit 0) when vars.issue_id is empty — manual CLI
## runs don't have a dispatcher-attached issue to transition.
## Routes through the dispatcher's HTTP API (the daemon's own Store
## instance) so its in-memory index stays coherent — an external CLI
## write would be clobbered by the daemon's next Claim/Release.
## ITERION_ISSUE_IDENTIFIER carries the short id; the port defaults
## to the studio's 4891 (override via ITERION_DISPATCHER_PORT).
## `|| true` keeps the workflow on the happy path if curl fails — the
## commits have already landed.
tool mark_issue_for_review:
  command: `if [ -n {{vars.issue_id}} ]; then ID={{vars.issue_id}}; ID_SHORT=${ID#native:}; PORT=${ITERION_DISPATCHER_PORT:-4891}; curl -sS -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"to":"review"}' "http://localhost:${PORT}/api/v1/native/issues/${ID_SHORT}/transition" 2>&1 | head -c 1000 || true; else echo '{"skipped":"no issue_id"}'; fi`
  output: mark_issue_output

## update_audit_cache (v3 — noop cache only): records the git HEAD the
## docs were aligned to at the configured cache path. The NEXT run's
## scan_hints compares it against the live HEAD and short-circuits the
## whole campaign (noop_skip) when nothing changed. The v2 per-doc SHA
## pre-verification cache is retired with the manifest machinery.
tool update_audit_cache:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, json, sys, pathlib, subprocess
ws = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else '.'
cache_path = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else ''
if not cache_path:
    print(json.dumps({'cache_path': '', 'git_head': '', 'skipped': 'no cache_path'}))
    sys.exit(0)
os.chdir(ws)
try:
    head = subprocess.run(['git', 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
except Exception:
    head = ''
out_path = pathlib.Path(cache_path)
out_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
with open(out_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as out:
    json.dump({'v': 2, 'git_head': head}, out)
print(json.dumps({'cache_path': str(out_path), 'git_head': head, 'skipped': ''}))
" {{input.workspace_dir}} {{input.cache_path}}`
  input: update_audit_cache_input
  output: update_audit_cache_output

## ─── Nodes : PR finalization tail (opt-in, feature-dev verbatim) ────

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output so the two outgoing
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"

## forge_auth_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-flight for the push. finalize_mr is an
## LLM agent; entering it with NO push credential mounted burns a whole turn
## just to rediscover, in shell, that it can't push. This tool answers "is
## there a push credential?" in ~100ms of subprocess so the graph only pays
## finalize_mr when a push can actually happen. python3 for safe JSON;
## read-only, idempotent.
tool forge_auth_probe:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
tok = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(tok) and os.path.getsize(tok) > 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'file:' + tok})); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    pass
for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
    if os.environ.get(v):
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'env:' + v})); raise SystemExit(0)
try:
    r = subprocess.run(['gh', 'auth', 'status'], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'gh host auth'})); raise SystemExit(0)
except Exception:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'available': False, 'reason': 'no forge_token secret, no *_TOKEN env, no gh host auth'}))
"`
  output: forge_auth_state

agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  tool_max_steps: 20

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow docs_refresh:
  entry: scan_hints

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/ —
  ## the campaign commits doc alignments in stride; the worktree +
  ## finalize FF keeps the operator's checkout untouched during the run
  ## and lands the series on a persistent branch on convergence.
  worktree: auto

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h"
    max_cost_usd: 60

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## NOOP short-circuit: docs already aligned to the exact current
  ## HEAD, clean tree, no explicit request → skip the whole campaign
  ## with zero LLM cost. Highest-priority guard; mutually exclusive
  ## with no_docs (noop requires doc_count > 0) and the else fallback.
  ## DEFAULT-CREATE: if the repo has ZERO docs in scope, bootstrap an
  ## initial set first (author_docs), then loop back to scan_hints
  ## (author_rescan) so the re-scan picks the new docs up and the
  ## normal campaign polishes + commits them. author_rescan(1) =
  ## exactly one authoring pass.
  scan_hints -> done when noop_skip
  scan_hints -> author_docs when no_docs with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    docs_dir: "{{vars.docs_dir}}",
    recently_changed_code_files: "{{outputs.scan_hints.recently_changed_code_files}}"
  }
  ## Each pass: the fresh advisory report is handed to the campaign as
  ## HELP (+ the previous gate's feedback — empty on pass 1,
  ## outputs.gate resolves nil-safe before the gate has run).
  scan_hints -> campaign else with {
    hints: "{{outputs.scan_hints.hints}}",
    hint_count: "{{outputs.scan_hints.hint_count}}",
    hints_note: "{{outputs.scan_hints.hints_note}}",
    recently_changed_code_files: "{{outputs.scan_hints.recently_changed_code_files}}",
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }
  author_docs -> scan_hints as author_rescan(1)

  ## Deterministic TRUTH gates after every campaign pass: writeable-set
  ## containment, then the build gate (skipped via verify_precheck when
  ## nothing build-relevant changed since the last green verify —
  ## outputs.verify_run then still carries the verdict being reused),
  ## then the continuation decision.
  campaign -> scope_check
  scope_check -> verify_precheck
  verify_precheck -> gate when reuse
  verify_precheck -> verify_build when not reuse
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged → transition the dispatcher issue (no-op without
  ## issue_id) and refresh the noop cache, then finish.
  gate -> mark_issue_for_review when converged
  ## Not converged → another pass through the loop head: a fresh
  ## advisory report (resolved hints + ledger entries retire) →
  ## campaign. A scope violation or build failure rides
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log on the scan_hints→campaign edge;
  ## green-but-more-work rides an empty log.
  gate -> scan_hints as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}")
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while work remains) →
  ## finish anyway, shipping what is banked (every committed alignment
  ## is real, landed work; the last campaign_output's drift_remaining
  ## tells the operator what is left).
  gate -> mark_issue_for_review

  mark_issue_for_review -> update_audit_cache with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    cache_path: "{{vars.audit_cache_path}}"
  }
  ## ── PR tail (opt-in, feature-dev verbatim) ──────────────────────
  ## Reached on BOTH finishes (converged and max_passes-exhausted): the
  ## banked alignment commits are real landed work either way, and
  ## finalize_mr refuses an empty PR + reports drift_remaining honestly
  ## in the body. The noop_skip path (scan_hints -> done) never gets here.
  update_audit_cache -> mr_gate
  mr_gate -> forge_auth_probe when open_mr
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr

  forge_auth_probe -> finalize_mr when available with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}",
    campaign_summary: "{{outputs.campaign.summary}}",
    drift_remaining:  "{{outputs.campaign.drift_remaining}}"
  }
  forge_auth_probe -> done when not available
  finalize_mr -> done
